write('081',['Distinguish agreement safety from progress liveness.','Explain why deterministic execution follows a committed command order.','State which failures a crash-fault consensus system covers.'],['L076–080: can a replica answer a stale read while all stored copies are durable?','L036: what is atomic within one database transaction?'],[
sec('problem','Three copies do not define one history',`Core 55 minutes: retrieval 5, mechanism 18, worked history 8, exercise 10, scheduled questions 10, review 4. A campus equipment service has one remaining camera. Two servers each read available=1 and each approve a different borrower. Both machines may durably store their answers. The problem is not missing copies; it is the absence of a common decision order that protects the one-loan invariant.

A replicated state machine separates two responsibilities. An ordering layer establishes a committed sequence of commands. Each replica's application state machine applies those commands, in that order, from the same starting state. If execution is deterministic, the resulting states agree. Determinism includes seemingly harmless operations: calling the local clock or generating a random identifier separately on every replica can produce different output from the same command. Include a chosen timestamp or identifier in the agreed command when the application needs one.

This does not require placing every large binary object into a consensus log. A metadata service might agree on ownership and object references while bulk bytes follow another durability path. Identify exactly which decisions require common order and which data can remain outside that authority.`),
sec('properties','Safety can hold while no request completes',`Agreement means replicas do not decide incompatible values for the same position. A state-machine safety property can say that two replicas never apply different commands at the same committed log index. Application safety adds domain invariants, such as never approving two current loans for one item. Consensus cannot rescue a state transition that is itself wrong: if every replica deterministically approves an overdraft, their agreement is perfectly consistent and the application is still incorrect.

Liveness asks whether requests eventually make progress under stated conditions. During a partition, a minority may refuse writes indefinitely while the majority continues. That is a loss of availability for minority-connected clients, not a safety violation. The safety argument must not depend on an operator confidently declaring a silent machine dead; it might merely be delayed.

The baseline in this module assumes crash/recovery faults, authenticated nonmalicious members, durable persisted protocol state, and a network that may delay, duplicate, reorder or lose messages. It does not tolerate arbitrary malicious replicas lying about their state. Progress also needs a sufficiently stable communication period and a functioning quorum. Saying “works under any failure” hides these boundaries.`),
sec('trace','From command to applied state',`Use a register-like state machine with an item and a request result table. Start with item42 unclaimed. Command at index 7 is reserve(item42, alice, requestA). It sees no current owner, stores alice, and records success for requestA. Index 8 is reserve(item42, bob, requestB). It sees alice and records a conflict. Every replica applies the same commands and returns the same results. A retry of requestA returns its stored result instead of making a second reservation.

The teaching transition below is executable Python but is an in-memory state-machine function, not a replication protocol:

~~~python
def reserve(state, request_id, item, actor):
    if request_id in state['results']:
        return state['results'][request_id]
    ok = item not in state['owners']
    if ok:
        state['owners'][item] = actor
    state['results'][request_id] = ok
    return ok
~~~

The production command contract must additionally fingerprint a request ID's payload, so reusing requestA for a different item is rejected. The log establishes order; the request table establishes retry identity; the transition protects the domain invariant. They solve different problems.

With five voting replicas a majority is three. The group can continue if any three voters can communicate under the protocol's progress assumptions. Two isolated machines cannot safely invent a separate authoritative history. That arithmetic describes one group, not unlimited availability of every dependency in the product.`),
sec('cost','Place coordination where the invariant lives',`Every globally ordered mutation pays communication, persistence and contention costs at its ordering authority. A tiny configuration service can accept that cost; a high-rate analytics stream may partition independent keys and accept weaker cross-key guarantees. Separate consensus groups can scale independent state, but an invariant spanning groups needs an additional coordination or compensation design.

A common misconception is that consensus means every replica is always identical. Followers can lag while committed history remains safe. Another is that leader election alone establishes safe state: the elected leader must also preserve previously committed decisions. L082 and L083 add the election and log rules that make those claims fit together. Optional depth: contrast crash-fault consensus with Byzantine agreement; the membership assumptions and quorum arithmetic are different.`)
],{title:'Agreed order precedes deterministic application',source:'flowchart LR\nC[Client command] -->|proposal| O[Consensus order]\nO -->|committed prefix| A[Replica A state machine]\nO -->|same prefix| B[Replica B state machine]\nO -->|same prefix| D[Replica C state machine]',caption:'Replicas may apply at different speeds, but a committed position must not mean different commands. The diagram does not show a complete consensus protocol.',steps:['Validate a command and its retry identity.','Establish a committed order.','Apply only the committed prefix in sequence.','Return a result consistent with that application.']},{prompt:'A five-voter group splits into components of three and two. Both contain a previously elected leader at different observed times. Identify which component can make new decisions, what the other should do, and why a shared deterministic but incorrect transition remains unsafe for the business.',rubric:['Calculates majority three.','Separates leadership belief from quorum authority.','Distinguishes protocol safety from domain invariants.'],solution:'Only the component able to obtain a valid three-voter quorum can commit new decisions, subject to election and log rules. The two-voter side must not acknowledge new authoritative writes merely because a node remembers being leader. It can reject or redirect, and only offer explicitly weaker reads if the contract allows. If every replica executes a transition that grants an already-owned item, they agree on a business error. Repair the state transition as well as the replication boundary.'},[
choice('q1','Agreement alone proves business correctness:',['Yes','No'],'No','Replicas can agree on an incorrect transition; invariants still belong to application logic.'),num('q2','How many votes form a majority of seven voters?',4,'votes','floor(7/2)+1 = 4.'),choice('q3','Which input threatens deterministic replay if chosen independently at each replica?',['An identifier already present in the command','The replica’s local random draw'],'The replica’s local random draw','The replicas may generate different values from the same ordered command.'),open('q4','When is refusing a minority write an intended behavior?','When acknowledging it would risk incompatible authoritative decisions during a partition; safety may require loss of local progress.')],[['Safety?','Nothing forbidden occurs, such as conflicting applied commands at one committed index.'],['Liveness?','Progress eventually occurs under stated operating assumptions.'],['Deterministic replay?','Same starting state and ordered commands produce the same results.'],['Majority of five?','Three voters.'],['Consensus and application invariants?','Consensus orders decisions; the transition must still protect the domain rule.']],'Agree on an order, execute that order deterministically, and state separately when progress is possible.',[raft]);

write('082',['Trace a term change and majority election.','Apply one durable vote per term and the log freshness rule.','Explain why timeouts suspect failure without proving it.'],['L081: why can a minority safely stop?','L027: why does a timeout not prove the remote action failed?'],[
sec('terms','An epoch identifies the authority being discussed',`Core 55 minutes: retrieval 5, mechanism 18, worked trace 8, exercise 10, questions 10, review 4. A Raft term is a monotonically increasing logical epoch, not a wall-clock second and not a guarantee that every machine observed the same events. A node can learn about a higher term through an RPC and move forward without seeing the intervening elections. Messages carry terms so recipients can reject obsolete authority.

Nodes act as followers, candidates or leader. A follower that does not hear from a leader within its election timeout becomes a candidate, increments its term, votes for itself, and requests votes. A candidate wins only with a majority in that term. Randomized timeout choices reduce repeated split votes; they improve the chance of progress rather than proving a crashed leader.

A five-node cluster might use different timeout values across nodes. Do not hardcode a universal millisecond value from a blog post: the design relationship is that ordinary broadcast/persistence latency should be comfortably smaller than election timeout, while timeout is acceptably small compared with a real failure interval. Excessively short timeouts convert transient scheduling or disk stalls into disruptive election churn.`),
sec('votes','A vote has durable consequences',`A voter grants at most one candidate a vote in a term and requires the candidate's log to be at least as up-to-date as its own. Protocol term and vote state must survive crash/restart before the vote is acknowledged. Otherwise a voter could vote, crash, forget, and vote again in the same term; the quorum-intersection argument would rely on a promise the system did not persist.

Freshness compares the last log term first, then the last index if terms tie. A candidate with last term 8 and index 20 is fresher by this rule than one with last term 7 and index 100. “Longest log wins” is therefore not the rule. This restriction connects elections to the preservation of committed history; L083 explains the log side of that connection.

Two majorities within the same fixed voter set intersect. Since an intersecting voter cannot give two distinct candidates its vote in the same term, two candidates cannot both obtain a majority for that term. This argument says at most one elected leader per term. It does not mean an old partitioned leader instantly learns it has been replaced in a later term.`),
sec('walk','A delayed leader returns',`Nodes A, B and C begin in term 4 with A leading. A is paused. B times out, starts term 5, persists its self-vote, and asks C. C sees term 5, validates B's log, persists its vote and replies. B now has two of three votes and becomes leader. A resumes still believing term 4. Its term-4 AppendEntries is rejected by B or C with term 5, and A steps down after observing the higher term.

Until A hears a newer term, it may retain a local leadership belief. That belief is insufficient to acknowledge arbitrary writes or provide an unqualified fresh read. A current leader also needs protocol evidence that its authority has not been superseded; safe read paths are more than reading a local variable named is_leader.

This Python comparison is a small executable predicate, not a full election implementation:

~~~python
def log_at_least_as_fresh(candidate, voter):
    # tuples are (last_log_term, last_log_index)
    return candidate >= voter

assert log_at_least_as_fresh((8, 20), (7, 100))
assert not log_at_least_as_fresh((8, 19), (8, 20))
~~~

If B and C both time out together, each can vote for itself and neither may win. A later randomized retry in a new term can resolve the split. A sequence of failed elections is a liveness symptom; inventing a majority rule exception to make a dashboard green would threaten safety.`),
sec('operations','Election health is observable',`Track term changes, leader changes, election durations, peer round-trip latency and persistence latency. Frequent elections can originate in overloaded disks or stop-the-world pauses, not just broken cables. Avoid making more nodes voters as a reflex: adding a voter increases replication/coordination work and can change quorum requirements. Nonvoting learners and membership transitions are separate mechanisms, introduced as optional depth in L084.

The main interview explanation should connect durable voting, majority intersection and candidate freshness. Naming follower/candidate/leader without showing why an old leader cannot safely act alone is insufficient. Distinguish a safe protocol that cannot currently elect from an unsafe implementation that elects quickly after forgetting its vote.`)
],{title:'A new term invalidates old authority',source:'sequenceDiagram\nparticipant A as A term 4 leader\nparticipant B as B candidate\nparticipant C as C follower\nNote over A: Paused\nB->>B: Persist term 5 and self-vote\nB->>C: RequestVote term 5\nC->>C: Persist vote if log fresh\nC-->>B: Vote granted\nNote over B: Majority elected\nA->>C: AppendEntries term 4\nC-->>A: Reject with term 5\nA->>A: Step down',caption:'A delayed node can retain old beliefs; term comparisons and quorum requirements prevent those beliefs from creating current authority.',steps:['Candidate advances and persists its term.','Voter checks durable voting and log freshness.','A majority elects one leader for that term.','Returning old authority is rejected.']},{prompt:'In five voters, candidate X has last-log (term 6,index 90) and candidate Y has (term 7,index 12). A voter ends at (term 7,index 10). Which log meets the freshness rule, and why must its vote be durable before reply?',rubric:['Compares term before index.','Does not infer election from one eligible vote.','Explains double-voting after crash.'],solution:'Y meets freshness because its final term equals the voter and its index is greater. X is older by log term despite having more entries. Eligibility for one vote is not election; Y still needs a majority in its current election term. Persisting the chosen term/vote before replying prevents this voter from forgetting after restart and granting a competing candidate another vote in the same term.'},[
choice('q1','Election timeout proves the current leader is dead:',['True','False'],'False','Delay, partition and pause are indistinguishable to the timeout observer.'),num('q2','Minimum votes to elect in five fixed voters?',3,'votes','A majority is floor(5/2)+1.'),choice('q3','Candidate freshness compares first:',['Last log term','Wall-clock timestamp','Log length alone'],'Last log term','Only equal last terms are compared by last index.'),open('q4','Why can two nodes believe they are leader without two valid leaders in one term?','A partitioned old leader can retain an earlier-term belief while another leader is elected in a newer term; authority and read/write rules must reject stale action.')],[['Term?','Logical authority epoch; not elapsed wall-clock time.'],['Votes per term?','At most one durable candidate choice per voter.'],['Freshness order?','Last log term, then last log index.'],['Randomized election timeout?','Reduces repeated split votes; helps progress, not failure certainty.'],['Old leader on higher term?','Updates its term and steps down.']],'A timeout starts a candidacy; durable votes, log freshness and a majority establish authority.',[raft]);

write('083',['Separate append, replicate, commit and apply.','Trace conflicting follower log repair without discarding committed history.','Explain current-term commitment and fresh-read requirements.'],['L082: what does candidate freshness compare?','L081: why may a follower lag without violating safety?'],[
sec('boundaries','Four states hidden by the word “written”',`Core 55 minutes: retrieval 5, mechanism 18, worked replication 8, exercise 10, questions 10, review 4. A leader can append a command locally without the command being committed. It can replicate that entry to another machine without every replica having applied it. A committed command can remain unapplied temporarily while a state-machine worker catches up. These are different boundaries; a response contract must say which boundary success means.

A Raft log entry records a term and a command at an index. The leader sends AppendEntries with the preceding index/term so a follower can check whether the new suffix connects to the expected prefix. A follower rejects a request whose preceding entry does not match. The leader then adjusts its view of the follower's progress and sends the needed entries. It is not sufficient to append blindly to whatever the follower happens to contain.

The leader tracks replicated positions and a commit index. The state machine applies entries in order up to that index. A database client expecting the mutation's computed result normally receives success after the relevant command is committed and applied. An API may expose a different acknowledgement contract, but it must not call a local append a durable consensus result.`),
sec('trace','A majority survives one follower being slow',`A three-voter group has leader A and followers B/C. All share entries through index 10. In term 6 A appends set(x,9) at index 11 and sends AppendEntries with previous=(10,5). B verifies its prefix, persists entry 11, and replies. C is unreachable. A plus B are a majority; because entry 11 belongs to A's current term, A can advance commitment through 11. It applies the command, returns success, and later tells C which prefix is committed when C reconnects.

If A crashes before a reply reaches the client, the client observes an uncertain outcome. It cannot conclude the command failed. The command may already be committed and later returned by a new leader. A stable request ID and stored result avoid applying a retry as a second logical mutation. Consensus protects the log decision, not the client's ability to know whether a lost response represented success.

Replication latency in this example depends on the leader's persistence and the fastest follower needed for a majority, plus application work. Waiting for the slowest of all three is a different, stronger acknowledgement choice with worse latency/availability. Do not report a production latency number without measuring storage and network behavior.`),
sec('repair','Repair an uncommitted suffix, preserve committed decisions',`Suppose C contains an uncommitted entry at index 11 from an earlier failed leader, while the newly elected leader's entry 11 differs. Matching the prefix and receiving the leader's conflicting suffix allows the follower to replace its uncommitted conflicting tail. This does not license deletion of a committed command. Election restrictions and the commitment rules are designed so later valid leaders contain already committed entries.

A subtle rule concerns entries from previous terms. A leader must not directly mark an older-term entry committed merely because it currently counts that entry on a majority. In Raft, majority counting directly commits an entry from the leader's current term; committing such an entry also commits its preceding prefix. This qualification is easy to omit in an interview slogan such as “three copies means committed.” It matters when leadership changes and logs contain different uncommitted suffixes.

Use the following as an annotated predicate sketch, not a complete implementation:

~~~python
def can_advance(candidate_index, current_term, entry_term, replicated_count, voters):
    return (entry_term == current_term and
            replicated_count >= voters // 2 + 1)
~~~

Real logic also tracks which followers have which entries, persistent state, ordered application, errors and membership. A convenient boolean helper does not implement the consensus protocol.`),
sec('reads','A committed write does not make every local read fresh',`A follower can return an old applied state after a newer command committed elsewhere. That may be allowed for explicitly stale-tolerant reads, but it does not implement a linearizable register. A leader can also be stale about its own leadership after a partition. Fresh reads need a protocol that establishes current authority and waits for the relevant committed state to be applied, or an explicitly justified lease with timing assumptions.

Monitor commit-to-apply lag separately from follower replication lag. A healthy quorum can coexist with a slow application state machine. Track pending proposals and retry rates during leader transitions; an uncertain response should prompt an idempotent retry, not a new business operation. Optional depth: study the paper's old-term commitment counterexample and label the exact term/index at every step rather than relying on memorized pictures.`)
],{title:'Success follows the agreed and applied boundary',source:'sequenceDiagram\nparticipant Client\nparticipant A as Leader A\nparticipant B as Follower B\nparticipant C as Follower C\nClient->>A: Command with request ID\nA->>A: Persist current-term entry\nA->>B: Append after matching prefix\nB->>B: Persist entry\nB-->>A: Replication acknowledgement\nNote over C: Unreachable\nA->>A: Commit with majority then apply\nA-->>Client: Application result',caption:'The diagram assumes a fixed three-voter group and a current-term entry. An acknowledgement from the network stack is not a follower persistence acknowledgement.',steps:['Append with term and index.','Replicate only onto the matching prefix.','Use the correct majority commitment rule.','Apply before returning this API’s result.']},{prompt:'A five-voter leader acknowledges after sending an entry to two followers, without waiting for persistence replies. It then crashes. Explain the unsafe boundary and repair it. Also explain why counting three copies of an old-term entry requires care.',rubric:['Distinguishes sending from durable receipt.','Requires majority with leader counted appropriately.','States current-term direct commitment rule.'],solution:'Sending proves only that messages were attempted; they may be delayed or lost and the followers may not have persisted the entry. The leader must use confirmed durable replication according to the protocol, establish valid commitment, and apply if its API promises an application result before success. In five voters the majority is three, including the leader when its own copy is durable. Raft directly advances by replica counting only for a current-term entry; a committed current-term entry commits its preceding prefix indirectly, so old-term copy counts alone are not a sufficient shortcut.'},[
choice('q1','A local leader append implies committed success:',['Yes','No'],'No','The entry may never reach the required quorum or survive a later legitimate leader.'),num('q2','In five voters, with leader durable, how many additional durable replicas are minimally needed for a current-term majority?',2,'followers','Leader plus two followers gives three voters.'),choice('q3','A conflicting suffix that may be replaced is:',['Any committed prefix','An uncommitted tail conflicting with the leader'],'An uncommitted tail conflicting with the leader','Committed decisions must remain preserved by subsequent valid leaders.'),open('q4','Why does a lost success response require idempotency even with consensus?','The write may have committed before the reply was lost; retrying under a new logical identity could execute the business command twice.')],[['Append versus commit?','Local presence is not an irrevocable agreed decision.'],['Commit versus apply?','Commit establishes order; apply changes the state machine in that order.'],['Prefix check?','Previous log index and term anchor the incoming suffix.'],['Direct replica-count commitment?','Requires a current-term entry in Raft.'],['Fresh local leader read?','Needs current-authority and applied-state guarantees, not merely a leader flag.']],'A safe result crosses persistence, agreement and application boundaries in the correct order.',[raft]);

write('084',['Explain the state represented by a log snapshot.','Reason about quorum overlap during membership changes.','Trace lagging recovery without losing retry identities.'],['L083: which prefix can a state machine safely apply?','L082: why must election and log evidence refer to the same voter configuration?'],[
sec('growth','Compaction replaces a proven prefix',`Optional advanced extension, 55 scheduled minutes. Retrieval 5, mechanisms 20, history 10, exercise 10, questions/review 10. A log that records every command forever eventually makes restarts and storage growth impractical. A snapshot is a compact representation of state after a specific applied committed prefix. It is not simply “copy the current dictionary” while concurrent updates continue.

Record the last included index and term along with the state needed to resume correct execution. If requests are deduplicated with a result table, that table or an equivalent retained identity mechanism is part of the correctness state. Dropping it while retaining only business balances can cause a retried pre-snapshot command to apply again. Snapshot content follows the state machine's contract, not merely the fields convenient to serialize.

Creating, storing and installing snapshots must handle partial files and crashes. A practical implementation writes a complete new artifact, validates it, and atomically publishes its metadata before discarding the log prefix it supersedes. Exact implementation details depend on storage; the key lesson is never to destroy the only recovery path before the replacement is valid.`),
sec('catchup','A follower behind the retained log needs state transfer',`Imagine the leader retains log entries after index 10,000, while a recovering follower only has index 2,000. Repeatedly asking it to append at index 10,001 cannot reconstruct the missing prefix. The leader transfers a snapshot representing the compacted state and its index/term, then sends later log entries. The follower must install an internally consistent state and continue after the represented prefix without replaying already included commands.

For a 200 MiB snapshot over an effective 20 MiB/s link, payload transfer alone takes about ten seconds. This ignores protocol overhead, disk writes and ongoing writes. If the leader generates 5 MiB/s of new log during transfer, roughly 50 MiB more log accumulates in that simplified interval. Recovery capacity must exceed continuing generation or the follower never catches up. These are planning calculations, not a measured benchmark.

A failed transfer should be retryable without treating half a snapshot as valid state. Track snapshot duration, bytes, install failures, retained-log pressure and post-snapshot catch-up lag. A tiny healthy quorum can still struggle operationally when replacement nodes repeatedly consume its disk/network budget.`),
sec('membership','Changing voters changes the proof',`The intersection of majorities is meaningful relative to a voter set. If old and new configurations are switched independently, one majority of the old set and another majority of the new set might make incompatible decisions. The extended Raft paper describes joint consensus, where an intermediate configuration requires majorities of both old and new voter sets before finalizing the transition.

Consider old voters A,B,C and new voters C,D,E. Old majority A,B and new majority D,E do not overlap. Immediately allowing each side to use its own majority creates two independent authorities. During a joint phase, a decision must satisfy both sets; A,B alone or D,E alone is insufficient. The transition itself is logged state, not an operator changing a text file separately on every host.

Some implementations support constrained one-at-a-time membership changes instead of the exact joint-consensus mechanism described here. Consult that implementation's supported process rather than improvising a generic “remove two, add two” operation. Adding a learner before promoting it can reduce catch-up disruption, but a nonvoting replica is not counted in a voting majority until the membership protocol changes its role.`),
sec('history','Keep the recovery story complete',`Worked failure: a leader snapshots account=100 at committed index 500 but omits the dedup result for request R that produced it. A client never received R's reply. After all old log entries are compacted, a restart installs the snapshot and sees R as new; retrying R credits the account again. The numerical state looked plausible, yet the snapshot did not preserve the full application contract. Include sufficient retry state or impose a documented retry-identity expiration policy coordinated with clients.

The common misconceptions are that snapshots can include uncommitted speculative state, that any convenient backup equals a log snapshot, and that resizing a cluster is merely a capacity operation. Each affects the safety proof. The core general route may proceed without this extension, but an advanced explanation should distinguish replicated-log recovery, independent disaster recovery backups, and membership authority.`)
],{title:'Joint configuration requires both majority conditions',source:'flowchart LR\nO[Old A B C] -->|logged transition| J[Joint old plus new]\nJ -->|old majority AND new majority| N[New C D E]\nA[Old-only A B] -.->|insufficient alone| J\nD[New-only D E] -.->|insufficient alone| J',caption:'The example highlights disjoint old-only and new-only majorities. It describes the joint-consensus model, not every implementation’s administrative API.',steps:['Begin with one agreed voter configuration.','Log the joint transition.','Require both old and new majority conditions.','Finalize the new configuration through the supported protocol.']},{prompt:'A snapshot is 120 MiB, effective transfer 12 MiB/s, and new log arrives at 3 MiB/s. Estimate the transfer floor and generated catch-up bytes. Name two nonbusiness-state fields that correctness may require in a snapshot.',rubric:['Carries MiB units consistently.','Separates floor from complete recovery time.','Includes log boundary and retry identity state.'],solution:'Transfer alone takes 120/12=10 seconds, generating about 3×10=30 MiB of additional log under the constant-rate assumption. Installation and catch-up add time, and continued generation must be considered during catch-up. Snapshot metadata needs a last included index/term so later entries connect to the correct prefix; the state may also need client request IDs/results to preserve deduplication. Those fields are correctness obligations rather than incidental cache data.'},[
num('q1','Payload floor for 200 MiB at 20 MiB/s?',10,'seconds','200/20 = 10; overhead and installation add time.'),choice('q2','A snapshot may safely replace:',['Any speculative state','A consistent applied committed prefix'],'A consistent applied committed prefix','Speculative uncommitted effects cannot become durable authoritative snapshot state.'),choice('q3','A joint configuration generally requires:',['Either old or new majority','Both old and new majorities'],'Both old and new majorities','The intermediate rule protects the membership transition.'),open('q4','Why include request deduplication state in a snapshot?','Otherwise a retried old request can execute again after its log and identity record are compacted.')],[['Snapshot boundary?','A specific applied committed prefix with index/term metadata.'],['Snapshot completeness?','Includes correctness state such as retry identities, not only business values.'],['Catch-up stability?','Effective recovery throughput must exceed ongoing work generation.'],['Joint consensus?','Intermediate decisions require majorities of both configurations.'],['Learner voter?','A learner does not count until safely promoted by the membership protocol.']],'Compaction preserves the meaning of the old prefix; membership change preserves the meaning of a quorum.',[raft]);

write('086',['Explain prepare/promise and accept/accepted in single-decree Paxos.','Show how a later ballot preserves an already chosen value.','State the asynchronous impossibility boundary without concluding practical consensus is useless.'],['L081: distinguish agreement from eventual progress.','L082: how does a durable promise contribute to quorum reasoning?'],[
sec('question','One value must survive changes of proposer',`Advanced extension, 55 minutes: retrieval 5, two-phase intuition 20, worked ballots 10, exercise 10, questions/review 10. Suppose several controllers propose a new owner for one partition. The goal is to choose one value so that future valid decisions for this same consensus instance cannot replace it with a conflicting value. Single-decree Paxos exposes this one-decision problem; building a replicated log needs many instances and additional practical machinery.

Distinguish proposers, acceptors and learners as roles, even when one process implements several. A proposer coordinates a ballot identified by a globally unique ordered number. Acceptors persist protocol state. Learners discover the chosen value. A value is chosen when a suitable majority of acceptors has accepted it, not when one learner has heard the news. Losing a reply therefore does not imply that nothing was chosen.

The two phases are easier to understand as preserving the obligations created by previous work. A new proposer first discovers and constrains what acceptors may do. It then proposes a value compatible with the information obtained. A higher ballot number is permission to coordinate a later attempt, not permission to discard an already chosen value.`),
sec('mechanism','A promise constrains future accepts',`In phase one, the proposer sends prepare(n). An acceptor can promise not to accept proposals with lower ballot numbers and reports its highest-numbered accepted proposal, if any. Once the proposer has a majority of suitable promises, it must choose the value from the highest accepted ballot reported by that quorum. Only if none reports an accepted value may it choose a new value of its own.

In phase two, the proposer sends accept(n,value). An acceptor may accept unless it has already promised to a higher ballot. A majority of accepts chooses the value. Promises and accepted records must survive restart. If an acceptor forgets these obligations, later quorum intersections do not carry the information on which safety depends.

The familiar slogan “majorities intersect” is incomplete. The intersection must reveal and preserve prior choices according to these rules. If the new proposer simply prefers its own value regardless of accepted history, overlapping majorities do not save the algorithm. Ballot comparison and value-selection rules do the work that transforms overlap into safe agreement.`),
sec('ballots','A concrete delayed-message history',`Acceptors A,B,C begin empty. Proposer P obtains promises for ballot 10 from A and B, then both accept value blue. Blue is chosen even if P crashes before any client sees a result. Proposer Q starts ballot 11 and obtains promises from B and C. B reports accepted=(10,blue); C reports none. Q must propose blue in ballot 11. Its preference for red is irrelevant for this instance.

Now imagine ballot 10 had reached only A. Q's quorum B,C sees no accepted value and may choose red. A's isolated acceptance was not a chosen value. This is not data loss of an acknowledged consensus result; it is a proposal that never crossed the required decision boundary. A client API should not promise chosen success after one acceptor.

For a five-acceptor group, any two majorities of three share at least one acceptor. The lower bound follows from 3+3−5=1. But the shared machine must obey durable promises and report its accepted history. This arithmetic is a tool for understanding the proof, not a replacement for the protocol.`),
sec('boundary','No universal termination guarantee in a fully asynchronous model',`The classic asynchronous consensus impossibility result concerns deterministic protocols with even one possible crash failure in a model where message delays are unbounded. A slow participant cannot always be distinguished from a failed one, and some admissible executions prevent a decision. It does not say that systems can never agree, nor that safety must be abandoned whenever a packet is late.

Practical protocols obtain progress under additional assumptions, such as a sufficiently long period of stable timing and leadership, or use randomization in appropriate models. Two proposers can repeatedly preempt one another with increasing ballots while safety remains intact. A stable leader is an important engineering mechanism for reducing that contention. Tail latency, durable storage latency and failure detection remain operational concerns.

Keep this extension at the level of obligations and one explicit history. Implementing a production Multi-Paxos system requires log positions, reconfiguration, recovery, compaction and client semantics beyond this bounded lesson. Do not claim the two-phase sketch is a deployable service.`)
],{title:'Later ballots inherit prior accepted obligations',source:'sequenceDiagram\nparticipant P as Proposer Q\nparticipant B as Acceptor B\nparticipant C as Acceptor C\nP->>B: Prepare ballot 11\nB-->>P: Promise; accepted 10 blue\nP->>C: Prepare ballot 11\nC-->>P: Promise; none accepted\nP->>B: Accept 11 blue\nP->>C: Accept 11 blue\nNote over P,C: Prior accepted value constrains new proposal',caption:'The proposer must use the highest accepted value from its phase-one quorum. A higher ballot alone does not authorize a conflicting value.',steps:['Gather a majority of promises.','Inspect reported accepted proposals.','Select the required prior value when present.','Obtain a majority of accepts.']},{prompt:'In A,B,C, ballot 20 chooses value east on A and B. A later proposer gathers promises from B and C at ballot 21. B reports east at20, C reports nothing. May it propose west? Explain the result if it had gathered only C’s promise.',rubric:['Preserves highest accepted value from quorum.','Requires majority promises.','Distinguishes one response from a quorum.'],solution:'With promises from B and C, the proposer must carry east forward because it is the value of the highest accepted ballot in that quorum. Choosing west would violate the phase-one selection rule and could conflict with a chosen value. C alone is not a majority of three, so its empty report does not authorize moving to phase two as if history were empty. Wait for a quorum or report lack of progress.'},[
choice('q1','A higher ballot permits replacing an already chosen value:',['Yes','No'],'No','Higher ballots must preserve accepted obligations discovered through the quorum.'),num('q2','Minimum overlap of two size-three sets within five acceptors?',1,'acceptors','3+3−5 = 1.'),choice('q3','Which state must survive acceptor restart?',['Promises and accepted proposals','Only a display of the current leader'],'Promises and accepted proposals','Forgetting durable protocol obligations can break safety.'),open('q4','What does asynchronous consensus impossibility say about practical operation?','Some fully asynchronous executions prevent deterministic termination with a possible crash; practical progress relies on additional operating assumptions, while safety can still hold.')],[['Chosen value?','A value accepted by the required majority for an instance.'],['Prepare promise?','Do not accept lower ballots; report highest accepted proposal.'],['New proposer’s value?','Use the highest accepted value reported by its prepare quorum, if any.'],['Stable leader benefit?','Reduces competing ballot preemption and aids progress.'],['FLP boundary?','No unconditional deterministic termination guarantee in the fully asynchronous crash model.']],'A new proposer inherits old obligations; a newer ballot cannot erase an earlier choice.',[paxos,raft]);

write('087',['Distinguish an ownership lease from resource-side enforcement.','Trace a paused worker after lease expiry.','Design monotonically ordered fencing and separate retry idempotency.'],['L082: a stale leader can retain an old belief.','L037: where must a concurrency check sit relative to the protected write?'],[
sec('stale','A lock response cannot stop a paused process',`Core 55 minutes: retrieval 5, mechanism 18, failure timeline 8, exercise 10, questions 10, review 4. A batch worker obtains an exclusive lease to update an object. It pauses for a minute because of scheduling or a long runtime pause. The lease expires and another worker obtains ownership. The old worker resumes with the bytes it was about to write. It can still send a request even though its local lease library once said “acquired.”

A lease bounds how long the coordination service recognizes ownership without renewal. It does not remotely erase instructions from a stopped process. Checking “is my lease valid?” immediately before a write leaves a gap: the process can pause after the check and before the resource commits the write. The protected resource must participate in preventing obsolete owners from changing authoritative state.

This distinction matters for object writers, job workers, scheduled compaction and controller reconciliation. Locks used only to reduce duplicate work can tolerate occasional overlap; locks used to protect correctness need a specific failure argument. Begin by saying which category the lock belongs to.`),
sec('fence','Move the final check to the authority',`A fencing token is an ordered ownership generation attached to every protected operation. When worker B obtains token 42 after A had token 41, the resource remembers the greatest accepted token. After observing42, it rejects a write with41. The comparison and the protected write must be atomic at the resource. If one replica checks while another writes without that check, the fence is decorative.

Equal tokens may represent multiple operations from one owner; whether to permit them depends on the operation protocol. A fence alone does not deduplicate retries within the same generation. Add request identities or version checks for that purpose. Likewise, a bare monotonically increasing counter does not solve resource authorization: only authorized owners should obtain/use tokens for the relevant resource scope.

The exact guarantee is worth stating carefully: once the resource has observed a newer generation, an older generation cannot overwrite it. A resource that has seen only41 cannot infer from the number alone that lease41 expired elsewhere. If rejecting all post-expiry operations even before any newer owner appears is required, the resource needs additional lease validation or revocation semantics. Avoid claiming that a token acts as a magical globally synchronized clock.`),
sec('timeline','Show the stale write being rejected',`At t0 A holds41 and computes a replacement index file. At t1 A pauses. At t2 the coordinator expires A's lease. At t3 B obtains42 and writes a correct replacement; the object authority atomically records max_token=42 with the new value. At t4 A resumes and submits its older output with41. The authority rejects it before changing bytes. The successful path is about the receiver's behavior, not about hoping A notices a lost lease.

The supplied deterministic lab in projects/labs/coordination.py models that receiver with FencedRegister. Run it using Python; it accepts42 and rejects a delayed41. It is an in-memory teaching model, not a distributed lock implementation. The important test assertion is that the protected value remains B's result after A attempts its write.

SQL-style predicate sketch for a resource with one row:

~~~sql
UPDATE protected_objects
SET value = :new_value, fence = :token
WHERE object_id = :id AND fence <= :token;
~~~

The application must check whether a row was changed, enforce ownership/authentication, and define equal-token retries. Inserts, multiple-row invariants and external file writes need corresponding transactional or storage-native support. Copying this query next to an unrelated filesystem write does not make the two atomic.`),
sec('alternatives','Choose a mechanism the resource can enforce',`If the target API supports conditional updates with an object version, optimistic concurrency may avoid a separate distributed lock. If it accepts an idempotency key for a business operation, retry safety can sometimes remove the need to exclude duplicate attempts. If it supports neither conditions nor fencing, a lock service alone cannot guarantee protection against an indefinitely delayed old owner.

Leases also need renewal and failure policies. A worker should stop initiating new work when renewal becomes uncertain, but the resource fence remains the final defense. Use bounded operation duration and cancellation for efficiency, not as the only correctness proof. Track lease-loss events, stale-fence rejections and repeated work so an operator can see churn.

A common misleading answer is “use Redis/ZooKeeper/etcd locks” without naming what checks the final write. Name the authoritative resource, the generation's scope, when it changes, and the atomic stale-owner rejection. Those details make the design reviewable.`)
],{title:'Lease expiry leaves a stale process; fencing protects the resource',source:'sequenceDiagram\nparticipant A as Worker A token 41\nparticipant L as Lease authority\nparticipant B as Worker B token 42\nparticipant R as Protected resource\nNote over A: Pauses beyond lease\nL-->>B: New ownership token 42\nB->>R: Write with 42\nR->>R: Store value and fence 42 atomically\nNote over A: Resumes\nA->>R: Delayed write with 41\nR-->>A: Reject stale generation',caption:'The stale write is rejected by the resource after it has accepted the newer generation. Lease expiration alone cannot stop A from sending.',steps:['Old owner pauses.','Authority grants a newer generation.','Resource atomically accepts the newer value and fence.','Resource rejects the delayed old generation.']},{prompt:'A lock service grants42 to B after A’s41 expires. The storage server ignores tokens, and both workers write files. Explain the failure and give two defensible repairs when the object API does or does not support conditional versions.',rubric:['Locates enforcement at storage.','Does not rely only on client lease check.','Distinguishes supported from unsupported guarantees.'],solution:'The storage server can accept A’s delayed write after B’s write, because the lock authority cannot revoke already executing code. If the object API supports version conditions, attach an expected version or supported fencing generation to an atomic write and reject stale conditions. If it has no conditional or fencing mechanism, place writes behind an authority that can enforce them or redesign the effect to be idempotent/commutative where possible. Merely checking the lease at A before sending still leaves a pause window; do not claim a correctness guarantee the target cannot enforce.'},[
choice('q1','Who must reject the stale owner for resource correctness?',['Only the former worker','The protected resource or its atomic write authority'],'The protected resource or its atomic write authority','A paused former owner can resume without observing its lost lease.'),num('q2','After accepting fence42, a request arrives with41. How many protected writes should this stale request apply?',0,'writes','The resource must reject the older generation before mutation.'),choice('q3','Fencing alone deduplicates repeated commands with the same token:',['True','False'],'False','Same-owner retries need a separate idempotency or version mechanism.'),open('q4','Why is a client-side lease-valid check insufficient?','The client may pause after checking and before its write commits, allowing a later owner to be overwritten unless the receiver enforces freshness.')],[['Lease?','Time-bounded recognized ownership, normally maintained by renewals.'],['Fence?','Ordered generation checked atomically by the protected resource.'],['Stale pause hazard?','Old code can continue after authority has moved to a new owner.'],['Fence versus idempotency?','Fence orders owners; idempotency identifies repeated logical operations.'],['No receiver support?','A separate lock cannot by itself prevent every delayed obsolete effect.']],'An owner may be stale; correctness lives in the resource’s atomic decision to accept or reject its generation.',[etcd]);

write('088',['Separate configuration authority from request-serving data paths.','Design revisioned conditional updates and resumable watches.','Recover after watch history is compacted.'],['L083: a local read may lag committed state.','L084 optional: what does compaction remove from historical access?'],[
sec('planes','Configuration has a different workload from ordinary requests',`Core 55 minutes: retrieval 5, mechanisms18, worked watch8, exercise10, questions10, review4. A fleet of API servers needs a routing policy and a list of healthy backend addresses. Configuration changes perhaps ten times a minute; the fleet handles thousands of ordinary requests each second. Sending every user request through the configuration database would turn a low-rate coordination authority into a high-rate dependency.

The control plane validates and stores desired configuration, versions, ownership and membership information. The data plane uses an applied local configuration to route or serve traffic. A failure of the control plane may delay new configuration while existing safe configuration continues serving. Whether that is acceptable depends on the data: a routing preference may tolerate staleness, while a revoked credential or safety shutdown may require a stricter policy.

Service discovery is also a contract about freshness, not a guarantee that every listed endpoint is alive. A record can outlive a crashed instance until its lease or health timeout expires. Clients still need bounded connection attempts and error handling. Do not confuse a directory entry with current end-to-end readiness.`),
sec('updates','Use revision checks to preserve operator intent',`Represent a configuration object as value plus monotonically increasing revision. An editor reads revision37, makes a change and submits compare-revision=37 with the new value. If another editor already produced38, reject the stale update rather than overwriting it. This is the same optimistic concurrency principle from L037 applied to operational state.

Validation belongs before publication: parse the schema, check ranges and referential constraints, authorize the actor, and record the change identity. Clients may apply a new configuration atomically to avoid a half-updated routing table. Keep the last known valid version available for recovery. Rolling back content should still produce a new revision; revisions order events, so changing the contents back does not mean decreasing the revision number.

Small configuration values suit a consensus-backed metadata service. Large binaries or model weights typically belong in object storage, with the control plane publishing a versioned reference and integrity digest. This splits agreement about “which artifact” from bulk transfer. Data-plane clients should verify the artifact before switching, and report desired versus applied version separately.`),
sec('watch','A watch is a change stream with a recovery position',`A watch avoids polling full configuration repeatedly. The client first reads a snapshot and its revisionR, installs it, then watches changes afterR. On receiving an event it applies the complete revision and records its progress. If the connection drops, it reconnects after its last applied revision. The exact API's snapshot/watch semantics must close the race between the initial read and subscription.

In etcd's documented API, watch events are ordered by revision and resumable within retained history; watch operations themselves do not provide the same linearizability contract as default key/value operations. This is why a client tracks revisions rather than treating every socket arrival as proof of a globally fresh state. A progress notification can be useful for tracking the stream's known progress, but it is not a universal wall-clock freshness guarantee.

Suppose a client applied revision120 and disconnects. The service compacts history through150. A resume from121 can no longer recover the missing history. The client should read a fresh complete snapshot atR, replace its local derived state consistently, and resume afterR. Blindly skipping to the newest watch event can miss a deletion or create an invalid combination of keys. Document what happens when the data plane cannot refresh at all.`),
sec('trace','A safe configuration rollout is observable',`Worked flow: an operator validates routes v2 against a schema, conditionally writes it at revision88, and records a change reason. Instance A downloads the referenced artifact, checks its digest, builds the new routing table off to the side and atomically swaps it in. Instance B fails validation locally and retains87 while reporting desired88/applied87/error. A rollout controller can halt or issue a new revision restoring the previous content. A “write successful” response alone does not prove fleet-wide adoption.

Monitor revision lag, refresh age, failed applications, endpoint connection failures and the fraction of instances still on old configuration. Protect control-plane writes more strongly than ordinary reads: a valid but malicious route update can redirect an entire fleet. Use scoped identities and audit records. Avoid putting secrets into globally readable discovery entries.

Common mistakes include treating watch streams as durable forever, assuming every disconnected node can replay any old cursor, using presence as authorization, and failing open on safety-critical configuration without an explicit product decision. Optional depth: specify different staleness budgets for routing preferences, feature flags and credential revocation.`)
],{title:'Snapshot plus revisioned watch feeds a local data plane',source:'flowchart LR\nO[Authorized operator] -->|conditional update at revision| C[Configuration authority]\nC -->|snapshot plus revision R| L[Local validated configuration]\nC -.->|watch events after R| L\nL -->|atomic version switch| D[Data-plane requests]\nD -->|applied version and errors| M[Operational visibility]',caption:'Ordinary requests use validated local state. Desired control-plane revision and applied data-plane revision are separate observables.',steps:['Validate and authorize a conditional update.','Read/install a complete snapshot with a revision.','Apply ordered changes and retain the last applied position.','On history loss, refresh a complete snapshot instead of guessing.']},{prompt:'A client last applied revision40, but history through60 has been compacted. It also failed to apply a newly downloaded artifact because the checksum differed. Describe recovery and what status it should expose.',rubric:['Uses full snapshot after compacted cursor.','Does not install invalid artifact.','Separates desired/applied revisions and refresh age.'],solution:'The old cursor cannot reconstruct removed changes. Read a fresh consistent snapshot with revisionR, obtain and verify any referenced artifact, and atomically install only a valid complete version before watching afterR. A checksum mismatch must retain the prior safe configuration or enter the explicitly chosen failure mode, not silently accept bytes. Report desired revision, actual applied revision, verification error and last successful refresh age so operators can distinguish authority publication from fleet adoption.'},[
choice('q1','A successful configuration write proves all servers applied it:',['Yes','No'],'No','Each data-plane instance must validate, fetch and apply the new revision; some may fail.'),num('q2','After fully applying revision88, the next watch starts after which revision?',88,'revision','Resume after the last completely applied revision, subject to the API’s cursor semantics.'),choice('q3','If required watch history is compacted, use:',['Only the newest event','A fresh complete snapshot and a new watch position'],'A fresh complete snapshot and a new watch position','Skipping missing events can omit deletions or combine incompatible state.'),open('q4','Why can control-plane outage be tolerable for routing but dangerous for revocation?','Last-known routing may continue useful service; accepting revoked credentials from stale policy can violate security, so staleness/failure policy must depend on the configuration’s meaning.')],[['Control plane?','Owns desired policy, identities, revisions and coordination.'],['Data plane?','Serves ordinary requests using applied configuration.'],['Conditional config update?','Compare the expected revision so a stale editor cannot overwrite newer intent.'],['Watch resume after compaction?','Refresh a complete snapshot and continue from its revision.'],['Desired versus applied?','Published configuration and successfully installed configuration may differ.']],'Publish desired state safely, apply it explicitly, and make stale or failed adoption visible.',[etcd]);

write('089',['Turn safety claims into deterministic history assertions.','Distinguish overlapping from ordered operations in a register history.','Inject a stale-owner write and preserve the failing trace.'],['L087: which component enforces a fence?','L076–083: why is an acknowledged write followed by an old read suspicious under a fresh-read contract?'],[
sec('harness','Test a claim you can actually falsify',`This 60-minute lab uses the prepared projects/labs/coordination.py file. Budget: read/retrieve10, trace prediction10, execute and modify20, evidence/tests15, reflection5. Python setup is separate. The supplied model is deliberately small: a fenced register and an exhaustive checker for at most eight completed register operations. It is not a Raft implementation or a general proof of a distributed system.

A safety test starts with an observable contract. For a single register, a completed write that precedes a later read in real time must be visible to that read unless another write intervenes. Concurrent operations allow more than one valid ordering. The test should ask whether one complete legal sequential explanation exists that respects real-time precedence, rather than demanding one arbitrary interleaving chosen by the test author.

Represent each completed operation with kind, invocation time, response time and value. Here write.value is the submitted register value; read.value is the observed result. Times are integers in a deterministic teaching schedule, not distributed wall clocks. The harness controls the schedule and knows the interval boundaries.`),
sec('execute','Run the included examples and inspect the counterexample',`From the repository root run:

~~~powershell
py -3 projects/labs/coordination.py
py -3 -m unittest discover -s projects/tests -p test_coordination.py -v
~~~

Use python or python3 if that is your installed launcher. The expected first output is: Safe history accepted; lost acknowledged write rejected; stale worker fenced. Four focused tests check the corresponding mechanisms. These tests were executed using the bundled Python interpreter on the target Windows workspace.

History A writes1 during interval[0,2], then reads1 during[3,4]. It has a legal explanation: write followed by read. History B writes1 during[0,2], then reads0 during[3,4], with initial0. It has no legal explanation under a linearizable register contract, because the read starts after the write completed and no intervening write restores0.

History C writes1 during[0,4] while a read returns0 during[1,2]. It may be legal: the overlapping read can be ordered before the write's effect. Rejecting all old values without checking overlap would create a false positive. The checker explores possible orders consistent with interval precedence, updating a model register along each candidate path.`),
sec('inject','Use deterministic failures before random chaos',`For the fencing trace, create a FencedRegister, write42 with worker B's value, then attempt41 with worker A's delayed value. The second call must raise PermissionError, and the register must still hold B's value. As a deliberate exercise mutation, remove the token comparison in a copy of the learner experiment and rerun the test. The test should now fail by allowing stale replacement. Restore the check after recording the counterexample.

This is failure injection because the test explicitly constructs the consequence of a delayed old owner, rather than hoping random sleeps happen to reproduce it. A real integration test would delay an actual worker or message at a chosen boundary, capture invocation/response records, and feed the resulting history into an appropriate checker. The small model tests the receiver invariant; it does not validate real network transport, disk durability or a coordinator implementation.

Avoid wall-clock sleeps in these unit tests. They are slow and nondeterministic, especially across Windows scheduling and loaded CI machines. Use a fake clock and explicit event order for unit histories, then add separately labeled integration and stress tests for real dependencies.`),
sec('interpret','A passing history is evidence with a scope',`The checker handles only completed reads and writes to one register and at most eight operations; its search can grow factorially. Pending operations, multi-key transactions, compare-and-set, client crashes and clock uncertainty require a richer model. A timeout in a real trace cannot simply be marked “write did not happen”; uncertain outcomes must be modeled explicitly.

Record the exact schedule, initial state, expected property and observed result with a minimal counterexample. If a failure disappears under retries, keep the original history rather than declaring the system healthy. A test suite that only checks the final value can miss an earlier impossible read, so inspect the sequence of observations as well as the final state.

Common mistakes are calling a simulation a production benchmark, equating no detected failure with proof, and accidentally testing a different consistency model from the product contract. The learning objective is to make one claim falsifiable and repeatable before expanding the implementation's complexity.`)
],{title:'Intervals constrain legal sequential explanations',source:'sequenceDiagram\nparticipant W as Writer\nparticipant R as Reader\nparticipant S as Register authority\nW->>S: Write 1 begins at 0\nS-->>W: Write completes at 2\nR->>S: Read begins at 3\nS-->>R: Returns 0 at 4\nNote over W,R: No overlap; old value violates this contract',caption:'With initial0 and no intervening write, a read invoked after write1 completed cannot return0 under the declared linearizable register contract.',steps:['Capture invocation and completion boundaries.','Derive real-time precedence for nonoverlapping operations.','Search for a legal single-register explanation.','Preserve a history with no valid explanation as a counterexample.']},{prompt:'Add a history with write1 [0,2], read1 [3,4], read0 [5,6]. Predict the checker result and explain why checking only the first read would miss the issue. Then run the existing matching test.',rubric:['Requires one ordering for the entire history.','Detects impossible later regression.','Uses an executed deterministic test.'],solution:'The checker must reject the history. The write completes before both reads, no later write restores0, and the reads are also ordered, so no legal sequential register execution returns1 and then0. Checking only the first read would pass and miss the later regression. Run test_one_shared_explanation_required in test_coordination.py and record the result; the important evidence is the three-operation counterexample, not merely a green test count.'},[
choice('q1','An old read overlapping an unfinished write is always invalid:',['True','False'],'False','It may legally be ordered before the overlapping write, depending on the complete history.'),num('q2','Maximum completed operations accepted by this teaching checker?',8,'operations','The bound keeps exhaustive permutation exploration small and explicit.'),choice('q3','A timed-out real write should automatically be modeled as absent:',['Yes','No'],'No','The write may have taken effect before its response was lost.'),open('q4','What does the fenced-register unit test not establish?','It does not validate a real coordination service, transport, disk durability or every concurrency history; it checks the resource-side token invariant under a deterministic schedule.')],[['History record?','Operation kind, invocation, response, and observed or written value.'],['Real-time order?','A completed operation precedes one invoked strictly afterward.'],['Overlapping operations?','May admit more than one legal sequential order.'],['Deterministic failure injection?','Place a fault at a known boundary and reproduce the same trace.'],['Passing tests prove what?','The tested model satisfies selected cases; they do not prove all executions safe.']],'A useful failure test names the contract, controls a boundary, and saves a minimal contradictory history.',[raft,etcd]);

